WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.569
As with any superhero movie, not everything
gets wrapped up in the closing minutes of

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:08.160
Into The Spider-Verse; after all, they need
to leave room for sequels.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.500
Here are the biggest unanswered questions
in Into The Spider-Verse.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.160
And obviously, swing on out of here if you
haven't seen the movie yet: spider-spoilers

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:15.660
ahead.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.960
Into The Spider-Verse might share a name with
the Spider-Verse comic crossover, but the

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.820
two stories don't have much else in common.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.199
For one thing, the big villains of the comic
book crossover are the Inheritors, a family

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.820
of sadistic vampires who prey on the life-force
of multiversal Spider-Men.

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:34.000
As you can tell from that description alone,
they were probably just too complicated to

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.390
use as the villains for Into The Spider-Verse,
or maybe the filmmakers wanted to save them

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.700
for a possible sequel.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.380
In fact, them showing up in a sequel would
certainly be dangerous enough to get the spider-gang

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.020
back together.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.740
At the end of the film, we see Miles reveal
his identity to his private school roommate

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.680
— a character whose name never actually
learn.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.220
Still, his roommate does bear a passing resemblance
to Ganke, Miles' best friend from the comics.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.900
Ganke's visual look and personality were repurposed
into Ned Leeds in Homecoming, so maybe the

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.860
filmmakers were trying to show the original
character some love.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.200
Or maybe Miles just figured he owed the truth
to his roomie:

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.040
"Do animals talk in this dimension?

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.080
Cause I don't wanna freak him out."

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.700
Like its live-action counterparts, Into the
Spider-Verse makes sure it's worth it for

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.000
audiences to wait until after the credits.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.060
The post-credits scene features Miguel O'Hara,
a.k.a. the Spider-Man of the distant year

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:27.560
2099.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.700
Miguel mentions that he's going to go back
to the beginning, and transports himself to

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.200
Earth-67, the home of the Spider-Man cartoon
of the '60s.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:40.360
The scene that unfolds between Miguel and
the '60s cartoon Spider-Man is hilarious,

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.240
but it begs the question of why Miguel bothered
to do it at all.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:48.200
Is it possible that the post-credits scene
represents Spider-Man 2099's first effort

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.620
to recruit a spider-army to face the looming
threat of the Inheritors?

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.980
Or maybe he just wanted to learn from the
best.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.420
"This time they won't get away.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.440
No one's gonna knock down bank doors while
I'm around.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.120
Loud noises interrupt my sleep!"

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.560
Or maybe we should assume this was just a
gag and not think too hard about it...

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.900
The main universe in Into the Spider-Verse
most closely resembles the reality inhabited

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.660
by Marvel's now defunct Ultimate line of comics.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.980
Villains like Prowler and Green Goblin more
closely resemble the Ultimate versions of

00:02:18.989 --> 00:02:23.400
the characters, and of course, Miles Morales
first appeared in the Ultimate comics.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.660
But Miles didn't stay in the Ultimate universe.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.610
As part of the line-wide event Secret Wars,
the Ultimate universe was destroyed, and Miles

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.950
became a part of the prime Marvel Earth, the
place that the majority of our favorite Marvel

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.160
characters call home.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:41.120
So what, if anything, could this mean for
the main universe of Into the Spider-Verse?

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.840
On one hand, there's no reason any follow-up
films have to mirror the comics in any specific

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.420
ways.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.379
On the other, it seems strange that we now
have a popular and lucrative Marvel franchise

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.600
tha seems to be set in a world that no longer
exists in the comics.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.440
Does this mean that the Ultimate Universe
will be resurrected in the Marvel comics?

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.480
Or will Miles' universe be threatened with
destruction once again?

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.819
One of the most subtle mysteries of Into the
Spider-Verse isn't how Miles Morales becomes

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.380
Spider-Man, but rather why.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:12.760
Miles is bitten by a radioactive spider that's
going through the same glitching that affects

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.000
the alternate Spideys.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.420
That suggests that the spider who bites Miles
is also from an alternate Earth.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.580
But how does the spider get there, and when?

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:25.400
The super-collider event that brings the alternate
Spideys to Miles' world happens after Miles

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.500
is bitten by the spider.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.620
The likeliest explanation is that, like Gwen
Stacy and most of the other spider-folks,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.940
the irradiated arachnid traveled not only
across dimensions but into the past.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.380
“I was blown into last week.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:43.600
Literally."

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.900
If so, then the fact that it bit Miles might
suggest something a bit more than an accident.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.670
It feels more like providence — like the
spider chose Miles specifically because it

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.250
sensed something about him.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.460
Is this something we might learn more about
in a sequel?

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.000
With all the alternate Spider-folk back in
their home realities and her own nephew gone

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.580
to the Great Beyond, will Aunt May continue
to help Miles now that he's taken up the mantle

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:07.580
of Spider-Man?

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.480
After all, she has access to his Spider-Cave
and all its cool suits.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.340
At the very least, someone's going to have
to teach Miles how to make more web fluid

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.200
so that he can keep swinging around New York.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.500
Not to mention that with the original Spider-Man
of Miles' world gone and Peter B. Parker returned

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.880
to his home universe, May could fill the role
of Miles' mentor.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.080
The last we see of Peter B. Parker, he's back
on his own Earth with a bouquet of flowers,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.200
hoping to reconcile with his now ex-wife Mary
Jane Watson.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.000
However, Peter and Mary Jane have a long,
stormy history, and there's no guarantee Mary

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.020
Jane will take him back.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.510
Even if she does, there are plenty of reasons
to think it's only a matter of time before

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:45.440
they split again.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.660
Part of the tragedy of their relationship
is that Peter's identity as Spider-Man is

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:54.280
part of why Mary Jane loves him, and it's
also the biggest threat to their relationship.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:55.380
So who knows?

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.420
There may be no happily-ever-after for these
two.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.560
“Only a friend, Peter Parker?"

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.600
"That's all I have to give."

